          <div class="tl">
            <div class="tl-item tle tle--lm is-key" data-kind="landmark" data-eord={0} data-beyond={lmBeyond('2026-08-07T01:38:24Z') ? '' : undefined} hidden={lmBeyond('2026-08-07T01:38:24Z')}>
              <div class="tl-date"><Timestamp iso="2026-08-07T01:38:24Z" /></div>
              <div class="tl__title">The vendor disputes the word "fallback", and is disputed back</div>
              <div class="tl-body">
                COLDCARD posts what it calls an <a href="/record/sources/coldcardwallet-2085541034243600805/">"Extremely important correction"</a>, addressed to Jack Mallers, whose post is not held here: "there wasn't a weak entropy fallback." The Yasmarang generator, it writes, was MicroPython's built-in general-purpose RNG introduced upstream in May 2018 and did not enter COLDCARD seed generation until the libngu migration of March 2021; the design intent was the opposite of a software fallback, with <code>MICROPY_HW_ENABLE_RNG=0</code> meant to disable that path; and what people call a fallback was "inherited behavior from the underlying platform that became active because of a link-time error". An hour later James O'Beirne, whose May 2025 audit account is in the 4 August entry above, <a href="/record/sources/jamesob-2085555839797166530/">replies that this is "wild hairsplitting and misdirection"</a>: the vendor did fall back to weak entropy, the Yasmarang implementation in libngu was weaker than MicroPython's because it used hardcoded constants, and the vendor was warned about the issue by him and by at least one other person four years before him. Both statements are held; this archive does not adjudicate between them, and the published accounts of the guard and its call sites are set out on <a href="/how-it-broke/">how it broke</a>.
