                In the same overnight exchange of replies the vendor says it has <a href="/record/sources/coldcardwallet-2085549723981672841/">"multiple pull requests under review and will have new versions coming out"</a>; that it is <a href="/record/sources/coldcardwallet-2085551118164132316/">"devastated"</a> and that "the hacker could have done the right thing and responsibly disclosed"; that <a href="/record/sources/coldcardwallet-2085551349962334324/">"very likely we won't be around"</a>, but that this is no reason to stop trying to give people the best firmware it can; and that <a href="/record/sources/coldcardwallet-2085572285608366139/">"the bug lived in public for 5yrs, even third party researchers didn't find it. We believe if took the lastest LLM models to find it"</a>, quoted as posted. Later that morning Cointelegraph <a href="/record/sources/cointelegraph-2085597045734203509/">reports, citing Bloomberg</a>, that Coinkite says it is working on a post-mortem rather than speculating on the extent of customer losses; the Bloomberg report itself is not held here.
